      stimulus: |
        Mia is a 19-year-old TikTok creator with 180,000 followers
        who posts daily short-form videos on student life and
        mental health. She has launched a small merch line (tote
        bags, hoodies, stickers) through a print-on-demand
        supplier that holds no stock of its own but prints each
        item only when an order comes in. Mia designs the artwork,
        her sister edits the videos, and her supplier handles
        production and dispatch directly to her customers. She is
        trying to work out how best to coordinate between herself,
        her sister, and her supplier so that nothing falls through
        the cracks when merch drops go live alongside video
        campaigns.
      question: |
        Name one cloud-based tool Mia could use to coordinate with
        her sister and her supplier, and explain how it would
        support the specific collaboration needs of her business.
      max_marks: 4
      mark_scheme:
        name_expected: |
          A specific named cloud-based collaboration tool suitable
          to the scale and needs of the business. Examples that
          would score: Google Workspace (Docs, Sheets, Drive),
          Microsoft 365, Notion, Trello, Asana, ClickUp, Slack,
          Discord, a shared cloud folder with specific permissions
          set. A generic answer of "a cloud tool" or "the cloud"
          does not hit this mark; the tool must be named specifically
          enough that the reader knows what the student means.
        explain_expected: |
          A correct statement of what the tool does and how it
          enables collaboration. The student should identify that
          cloud-based tools let multiple people access the same
          content from different locations and devices without
          having to email files back and forth. For the example
          tools above, credit is given for explaining the specific
          collaboration mechanism: Google Docs lets multiple people
          edit the same document at the same time; Notion combines
          documents, tasks, and databases in one shared workspace;
          Trello uses cards on shared boards to track work;
          Slack/Discord provides channels for threaded chat.
        impact_expected: |
          The concrete consequence for Mia's business must be
          described, linked to the scenario's specific problems
          (coordinating three separate parties across creative,
          logistics, and family/personal relationships, and
          aligning merch drops with video campaigns). Examples
          that would score: fewer missed merch drop deadlines
          because everyone sees the same campaign calendar;
          faster turnaround on artwork approvals because her
          supplier can comment directly on the shared design;
          fewer duplicate or lost messages because conversation
          is organised by channel or board rather than spread
          across texts and DMs. Generic claims like "it makes
          work easier" without link to the scenario do not hit
          this mark.
      exemplar_answer: |
        Mia could use Notion as her central cloud-based
        collaboration tool. Notion combines shared documents,
        task boards, and simple databases in one workspace that
        she, her sister, and her supplier can all access through
        the same browser-based interface, with different permission
        levels for each collaborator so that her supplier sees
        only the artwork and order information, not her personal
        content planning.

        Using Notion would solve the specific coordination problem
        in the scenario. She can maintain a single merch drop
        calendar that lines up with her video schedule, so her
        sister knows which videos need to go live on which day and
        her supplier knows when demand will spike. Artwork files
        can sit alongside the campaign they belong to, so nothing
        gets lost in message threads. Because Notion is cloud-based,
        all three people can update and check the same information
        from their phones without needing to be in the same place,
        which matters given her supplier is a separate business and
        her sister works around her own studies.
