## What this topic asks of you

The Pearson spec splits this section into two distinct capabilities,
and you need to be able to talk about each one on its own terms:

**One:** how digital enables automated stock and inventory control,
and specifically how software, hardware, processes, and inter-system
communication work together to make that control automatic.

**Two:** how traditional and cloud-based technologies support
communication and collaboration with stakeholders, both internal
(people inside the business) and external (suppliers, delivery
partners, customers).

These are separate spec points. Do not blur them.

## Automated stock control: the four ingredients

A stock control system that deserves the word "automated" has to
combine four ingredients. If one is missing, the system still works,
it just works manually or with gaps.

**Software.** The programs that record stock levels, trigger
reorder points, produce reports, and display information on a
dashboard. Every piece of business logic lives in software.

**Hardware.** The physical devices that capture real-world events
and feed them into the software. Barcode scanners, weight sensors,
RFID readers, point-of-sale terminals. Without hardware, a human
has to type every change, and "automated" becomes aspirational.

**Processes.** The rules that describe what the system does when
events happen. When stock falls below five units, reorder. When an
item expires, flag for removal. When a courier scans a parcel as
delivered, mark the order complete. Processes are the logic that
software and hardware enact.

**System communication.** The way the four different parts talk to
each other. A barcode scanner captures a sale, the scanner sends
that data to the central database over the network, the database
updates stock levels, the dashboard reflects the new total, the
reorder rule triggers if a threshold is crossed. All of this happens
through system communication. Break the network and the automation
stops.

When a Paper 2 question asks you about automated stock control, you
are expected to be able to talk about all four, not just name one
or two. A strong answer walks a specific event (a customer buys an
item) through the full chain.

## Traditional versus cloud-based collaboration

For most of business history, collaboration happened through
traditional means: printed documents, in-person meetings, phone
calls, desktop software that saved files to local hard drives, email
with attachments.

Traditional tools still work, and they still matter. Many
organisations have good reasons to keep some work local (security,
regulation, legacy software). Traditional collaboration is not
obsolete.

**Cloud-based tools** work differently. The data and the software
live on remote servers accessed over the internet. Multiple people
can work on the same file at the same time. Updates from one user
appear to all others in seconds. Access follows the person, not the
machine. Examples include Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, Notion,
Trello, Slack, Dropbox.

**Key differences that matter on the exam:**

- **Location independence.** Cloud lets collaborators work from
  different places without sending files back and forth.
- **Real-time co-editing.** Two people editing the same document at
  the same time is native to cloud tools and impossible with
  traditional desktop software.
- **Version control.** Cloud tools automatically track who changed
  what and when; traditional tools typically leave this to manual
  file naming.
- **Access control.** Cloud tools make it straightforward to grant
  specific permissions to specific people (this person can edit,
  this person can comment, this person can only view).
- **Dependency on connectivity.** Cloud tools need a working internet
  connection; traditional desktop tools work offline.
- **Recurring cost.** Cloud tools are usually subscription-based;
  traditional software is often bought once outright.

## Internal versus external stakeholders

The spec is explicit that collaboration happens with both internal
and external stakeholders. A good answer on this topic mentions both.

**Internal stakeholders** are people inside the business. In a
large company: staff, managers, executives, other teams. In a
one-person business: there are no internal stakeholders in the
strict sense, though family members or employees who help out
function as internal.

**External stakeholders** are people or organisations outside the
business whose work the business depends on or affects. Suppliers,
delivery partners, customers, regulators, investors, accountants.

The reason exams push this distinction is that the tools you pick
often differ. Internal collaboration might use a company's internal
chat system (Slack, Teams). External collaboration with a supplier
might happen through shared cloud folders with controlled access,
or through the supplier's own portal.

## Small-business reality

Paper 2 scenarios regularly feature small businesses, and the
tools a small business actually uses are different from the
enterprise tools you might see mentioned in generic business
reading. Do not assume every small business runs SAP. Most run on
Google Workspace or Microsoft 365, a shared spreadsheet or two,
and WhatsApp for quick messaging. The question you should ask on a
small-business scenario is "what is the cheapest and simplest tool
that genuinely supports the collaboration this business needs?",
not "what is the most sophisticated tool available?"

The assessment below works through one such scenario.
